ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE SYNTHETIC BIOLOGY PROMOTION ACT

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Synthetic Biology Promotion Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

CHAPTER II SYSTEM FOR FOSTERING AND PROMOTING SYNTHETIC BIOLOGY

Article 2 (Formulation of Master Plans for Fostering Synthetic Biology)
(1) To formulate a master plan for fostering synthetic biology under Article 5 (1) of the Synthetic Biology Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") (hereinafter referred to as "master plan"), the Minister of Science and ICT shall prepare guidelines for formulating the master plan after consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and notify those heads of the guidelines.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall prepare plans for the fields under their jurisdiction in accordance with the guidelines for formulating the master plan notified pursuant to paragraph (1), and submit them to the Minister of Science and ICT within 30 days from the date of notification.
(3) Where the plans for the fields under the respective agencies' jurisdiction submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) overlap or conflict, the Minister of Science and ICT may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to revise, supplement or adjust those plans.
(4) The Minister of Science and ICT shall consolidate the plans for the fields under the respective agencies' jurisdiction submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) (including plans revised, supplemented or adjusted pursuant to paragraph (3)) to prepare a draft master plan, notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies thereof, and solicit their opinions.
(5) "Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter part of Article 5 (3) of the Act means the following matters:
1. Matters that do not affect the purposes or direction of the master plan and for which there are clear grounds for amendment;
2. Matters constituting calculation errors, clerical errors, omissions or comparable manifest errors.
(6) Where a master plan is finalized or amended pursuant to Article 5 (3) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT shall, without delay, notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies of its details and announce them on the website of the Ministry of Science and ICT.

Article 3 (Formulation of Annual Implementation Plans)
(1) To efficiently formulate annual implementation plans under Article 6 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plans"), the Minister of Science and ICT shall notify the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to submit the results of carrying out the preceding year's implementation plan and the implementation plan for the following year for the fields under their respective jurisdiction.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies who receive notification under paragraph (1) shall submit to the Minister of Science and ICT, by January 15 of each year, the results of carrying out the preceding year's implementation plan and the implementation plan for the following year for the fields under their respective jurisdiction.
(3) The Minister of Science and ICT shall formulate an implementation plan reflecting the matters submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) and, pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act, report by March 31 of each year the results of carrying out the preceding year's implementation plan and the implementation plan for the following year to the Comprehensive Biotechnology Policy Deliberative Council under Article 7 of the Biotechnology Support Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Deliberative Council").
(4) Where the head of a relevant central administrative agency needs to amend the details of an implementation plan reported to the Deliberative Council pursuant to paragraph (3), the head may request such amendment from the Minister of Science and ICT.
(5) Upon receiving a request for amendment under paragraph (4), the Minister of Science and ICT may amend the implementation plan if the Minister deems it necessary, taking into consideration its connection with the master plan and other matters.
(6) Where the Minister of Science and ICT amends an implementation plan pursuant to paragraph (5), the Minister shall report the amended implementation plan to the Deliberative Council.

Article 4 (Composition and Operation of the Synthetic Biology Working Committee)
(1) The Synthetic Biology Working Committee under Article 7 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Working Committee") shall handle the following matters:
1. Preliminary review and adjustment of agenda items to be submitted to meetings of the Deliberative Council;
2. Affairs delegated by the Deliberative Council;
3. Selection of synthetic biology technologies to be subject to technology impact assessment and technology level assessment conducted pursuant to Article 11 of the Act;
4. Review of designation of hub institutions for synthetic biology research and development under Article 15 of the Act;
5. Review of designation of operating institutions under Article 19 (2) of the Act;
6. Review of synthetic biology research and development guidelines under Article 25 (1) of the Act.
(2) The Working Committee shall be composed of not more than 20 members, including one chairperson, with due consideration given to gender.
(3) The chairperson of the Working Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "chairperson" in this Article) shall be a person designated by the Minister of Science and ICT from among public officials in the Senior Civil Service responsible for synthetic biology policy in the Ministry of Science and ICT. Members of the Working Committee shall be persons appointed or commissioned by the Minister of Science and ICT from among persons falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Public officials of division-director level or higher responsible for duties related to synthetic biology policy among public officials belonging to the Ministry of Finance and Economy, Ministry of Science and ICT, Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, Ministry of Trade and Industry, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, and Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, who are designated by the heads of their respective central administrative agencies;
2. Persons engaged in industry, academia, research or other sectors who have extensive knowledge and experience in the field of synthetic biology.
(4) Where the chairperson deems it necessary for deliberation on an agenda item, the chairperson may have public officials belonging to relevant central administrative agencies or relevant experts who are not members attend a meeting to hear their opinions.
(5) Members commissioned pursuant to paragraph (3) 2 shall serve a term of two years.
(6) The Working Committee shall have one executive secretary, designated by the chairperson from among public officials belonging to the Ministry of Science and ICT.
(7) In addition to the matters prescribed in paragraphs (2) through (6), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Working Committee shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Working Committee.

Article 5 (Surveys of Current Status and Projections and Forecasts of Development Directions)
(1) To ascertain the domestic and international status of synthetic biology and project and forecast its development directions pursuant to Article 9 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT shall conduct annual surveys and analyses of the following matters and reflect the results in the master plan and implementation plans:
1. The status of technologies, industries, policies and systems related to synthetic biology;
2. The status of infrastructure related to synthetic biology, including personnel, facilities and equipment;
3. The status and operation of safety management systems related to synthetic biology;
4. The status of public awareness and acceptance of synthetic biology;
5. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Science and ICT for fostering synthetic biology.
(2) Where necessary to conduct surveys of current status and projections and forecasts of development directions, the Minister of Science and ICT may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies or institutions or organizations related to synthetic biology research and development to provide data.

Article 6 (Subjects and Methods of Compiling Statistics)
(1) Statistics compiled pursuant to Article 10 (1) of the Act shall include the following matters:
1. The status of research and development and industries related to synthetic biology;
2. The size and distribution of the professional workforce related to synthetic biology;
3. Scientific and technological achievements in the field of synthetic biology, including papers and patents, and the status of utilization of those achievements;
4. The status of establishment, operation and utilization of public biofoundries under Article 19 (1) of the Act;
5. The status of establishment and operation of safety management systems related to synthetic biology under Article 26 (1) of the Act;
6. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Science and ICT to efficiently formulate policies for fostering synthetic biology.
(2) Statistics under paragraph (1) may be compiled through literature surveys, field surveys, questionnaire surveys, solicitation of expert opinions and other methods, and electronic means such as telephone, information and communications networks or electronic mail may be used where necessary.

Article 7 (Technology Impact Assessment)
(1) The Minister of Science and ICT shall, pursuant to Article 11 (1) of the Act, conduct a technology impact assessment of synthetic biology technologies selected following review by the Working Committee.
(2) A technology impact assessment shall include the following matters:
1. The impact of the development and industrialization of the relevant synthetic biology technology on the nation and society as a whole;
2. The impact of the relevant synthetic biology technology on the economy, society, culture, ethics, and the environment;
3. Adverse effects that the relevant synthetic biology technology may cause and measures to address them;
4. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Science and ICT.
(3) The Minister of Science and ICT shall finalize the results of the technology impact assessment through deliberation by the Deliberative Council and notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies of the results.
(4) The heads of the relevant central administrative agencies shall formulate measures to minimize any adverse effects that synthetic biology technologies may cause, including reflecting the results of the technology impact assessment under paragraph (3) in national research and development projects under item 1 of Article 2 of the National Research and Development Innovation Act (hereinafter referred to as “national research and development projects”).

Article 8 (Technology Level Assessment)
(1) The Minister of Science and ICT shall, pursuant to Article 11 (2) of the Act, conduct a technology level assessment of synthetic biology technologies selected following review by the Working Committee.
(2) A technology level assessment shall include the following matters:
1. Comparison of the level of a particular technology in advanced countries and in the Republic of Korea;
2. Technology levels at present and in five years, and the time required for technology development;
3. Major factors that have contributed to achieving the current technology level;
4. Measures to improve the level of the relevant technology;
5. Technical, industrial, and economic obstacles to development of the relevant technology;
6. Policy directions, division of roles, and directions for resource allocation for development of the relevant technology.
(3) The Minister of Science and ICT shall finalize the results of the technology level assessment through deliberation by the Deliberative Council and notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies of the results.
(4) The heads of the relevant central administrative agencies shall reflect the results of the technology level assessment under paragraph (3) in policy formulation for the field of synthetic biology and in national research and development projects.

Article 9 (Composition and Operation of the Synthetic Biology Development Council)
(1) The Synthetic Biology Development Council under Article 12 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Development Council”) shall consist of no more than 20 members, including one chairperson, with due consideration given to gender.
(2) Members of the Development Council shall be commissioned by the Minister of Science and ICT from among private-sector experts in industry, academia, research, and other sectors who have extensive knowledge and experience in synthetic biology.
(3) The chairperson of the Development Council shall be elected by and from among its members.
(4) Matters concerning the composition and operation of the Development Council, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined by the chairperson of the Development Council following a resolution of the Development Council.

Chapter 3 Promotion of and Support for Synthetic Biology Research and Development

Article 10 (Preparation of Synthetic Biology Technology Roadmaps)
(1) The Minister of Science and ICT shall prepare a comprehensive technology roadmap for the field of synthetic biology under Article 14 (3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “technology roadmap” in this Article) every five years and notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies thereof.
(2) A technology roadmap shall include the following matters:
1. Development trends and prospects for advancement in major areas of synthetic biology technology;
2. Demand and market prospects for major synthetic biology technologies;
3. Analysis of research capabilities and constraints concerning synthetic biology technology development;
4. Analysis of gaps in synthetic biology technology compared with advanced countries and strategies to address them;
5. The timing and feasibility of realizing major synthetic biology technologies;
6. Analysis of patents relating to major synthetic biology technologies.
(3) The Minister of Science and ICT may, where necessary to prepare a technology roadmap, request research institutions, universities, enterprises participating in national research and development projects, and other relevant entities to submit related materials.

Article 11 (Requirements and Procedures for Designation of Hub Institutions for Synthetic Biology Research and Development, Etc.)
(1) Hub institutions for synthetic biology research and development under Article 15 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “hub institutions”) shall be designated from among the following entities. In such cases, the Minister of Science and ICT shall formulate and publicly announce a plan for designation of hub institutions.
1. Schools under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, graduate schools under Article 29 of that Act, and graduate school universities under Article 30 of that Act;
2. Government-funded research institutions in the field of science and technology under Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutions, Etc.;
3. National university hospitals under the Act on the Establishment of National University Hospitals, national university dental hospitals under the Act on the Establishment of National University Dental Hospitals, Seoul National University Hospital under the Act on the Establishment of Seoul National University Hospital, and Seoul National University Dental Hospital under the Act on the Establishment of Seoul National University Dental Hospital;
4. Specific research institutions under Article 2 of the Support of Specific Research Institutions Act;
5. The Korea Institute of Ocean Science and Technology under the Korea Institute of Ocean Science and Technology Act;
6. National and public research institutions.
(2) The requirements for designation as a hub institution shall be as follows. In such cases, detailed criteria for the designation requirements shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.
1. Possession of research infrastructure, including personnel, facilities, equipment, and information, sufficient to perform the functions specified in the items of Article 15 (2) of the Act;
2. Establishment and operation of a system for cooperation among industry, academia, and the research community related to synthetic biology;
3. Financial capacity necessary to operate a hub institution;
4. Possession of outstanding research achievements related to synthetic biology.
(3) An entity seeking designation as a hub institution shall meet all the designation requirements under paragraph (2) and submit to the Minister of Science and ICT an application form prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT, attaching the following documents:
1. A hub institution operation plan;
2. Documents proving that all the requirements specified in the items of paragraph (2) are met.
(4) The Minister of Science and ICT shall, after conducting a documentary review and an on-site inspection, decide whether to designate a hub institution following review by the Working Committee.
(5) Where the Minister of Science and ICT designates a hub institution under Article 15 (1) of the Act or revokes its designation under Article 15 (4) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly notify that fact in the Official Gazette and on the website of the Ministry of Science and ICT.
(6) Matters necessary for designation and operation of hub institutions, including designation and review procedures, operation, and performance evaluation, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Chapter IV Establishment of a Research Base and Creation of a Research Environment for Synthetic Biology

Article 12 (Establishment and Operation of Public Biofoundries)
(1) When establishing a public biofoundry under Article 19(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "public biofoundry"), the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall take into consideration biosafety and biosecurity, the sustainability of its operation, and linkages among public biofoundries.
(2) The head of an operating institution under Article 19(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "operating institution") shall prepare a performance report on the operation of the public biofoundry and submit it to the head of the relevant central administrative agency within 60 days after the end of each fiscal year.
(3) Matters necessary for the establishment and operation of public biofoundries, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Article 13 (Requirements and Procedures for Designation of Operating Institutions of Public Biofoundries, Etc.)
(1) The requirements for designation as an operating institution shall be as follows. In such cases, detailed standards for the designation requirements shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.
1. It shall be an institution or organization that performs activities relating to research and development in, and the utilization of, synthetic biology;
2. It shall have a dedicated organization and specialized personnel necessary to support activities relating to research and development in, and the utilization of, synthetic biology in its capacity as an operating institution of a public biofoundry;
3. It shall possess the space, facilities, equipment, and operational capabilities necessary for the operation of a public biofoundry;
4. It shall have established a safety and security management system for the operation of a public biofoundry;
5. It shall have internal regulations (meaning articles of incorporation or equivalent regulations) governing the personnel, operation, etc. of the dedicated organization for the public biofoundry.
(2) A person seeking designation as an operating institution pursuant to Article 19(2) of the Act shall satisfy all the designation requirements under paragraph (1) and apply for designation as an operating institution to the head of the relevant central administrative agency by submitting the application form prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT, accompanied by the following documents:
1. An operating plan for the operating institution;
2. Documents proving that all the requirements in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) have been satisfied.
(3) Upon receiving an application for designation as an operating institution under paragraph (2), the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall examine its suitability through a document review, an on-site inspection, etc., and determine whether to designate the applicant as an operating institution through a review by the Working Committee and deliberation by the Deliberative Council.
(4) Where the head of the relevant central administrative agency designates an operating institution pursuant to paragraph (3), the head shall publicly notify that fact in the Official Gazette and on the website of the relevant central administrative agency.

Article 14 (Use of Public Biofoundries, Etc.)
(1) A person seeking to use a public biofoundry (hereinafter referred to in this Article as a "user") shall apply to the head of the operating institution for use of the public biofoundry.
(2) Upon receiving an application for use under paragraph (1), the head of the operating institution shall review its suitability, including the priority of use, and then notify the user of the result.
(3) Pursuant to the latter part of Article 19(3) of the Act, the head of the operating institution shall require users to bear the costs incurred in using the public biofoundry (hereinafter referred to as "usage fees") in accordance with the standards for calculating usage fees prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.
(4) Notwithstanding paragraphs (2) and (3), the head of the operating institution may adjust the priority of use or grant an exemption from all or part of the usage fees where the Minister of Science and ICT determines that a situation constitutes a national emergency, such as an outbreak of an infectious disease or a crisis involving exports, imports, and supply chains.
(5) Pursuant to Article 19(5) of the Act, the head of the operating institution shall apply the usage fees toward the costs necessary for the establishment, operation, and joint use of the public biofoundry.
(6) Matters necessary for the use of public biofoundries, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Article 15 (Procedures for Joint Use of Synthetic Biology Research Data, Etc.)
(1) To jointly use synthetic biology research data generated or acquired through the use of a public biofoundry pursuant to Article 20(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to in this Article as "research data"), an operating institution shall conclude an agreement on the joint use of research data with the synthetic biology research entity, including the following matters (hereinafter referred to in this Article as a "joint use agreement"):
1. Types of research data;
2. The entity to which the research data belong;
3. The scope of joint use of research data (including research data intended to be provided to other synthetic biology research entities).
(2) Where a joint use agreement under paragraph (1) has been concluded, consent under Article 20(2) of the Act shall be deemed to have been obtained.
(3) Matters necessary for the use of research data, such as the types of research data to be jointly used and the methods of registering and storing research data, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Article 16 (Training and Securing Specialized Personnel)
"High schools prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 1 of Article 23(2) of the Act means the following high schools:
1. Among general high schools under subparagraph 1 of Article 76-3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act, high schools that establish and operate departments designated by the superintendent of education as departments for the purpose of fostering talent in industrial fields;
2. High schools tailored to industrial demand under Article 90(1)10 of the Enforcement Decree of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act;
3. Among specialized high schools under Article 91(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act, high schools whose purpose is to foster talent in industrial fields.

CHAPTER 5 RESPONSIBLE MANAGEMENT OF SYNTHETIC BIOLOGY, ETC.

Article 17 (Contents of Synthetic Biology Research and Development Guidelines, Etc.)

(1) When formulating synthetic biology research and development guidelines under Article 25 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "research and development guidelines"), the Minister of Science and ICT shall prepare draft research and development guidelines through the procedures under paragraph (2) of that Article, finalize them following review by the Working Committee and deliberation by the Deliberative Council, and notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies of the finalized contents.

(2) Research and development guidelines shall include the following matters:

1. Basic principles for ensuring the safety of synthetic biology research and development;

2. Requirements to be observed to prevent biological risks posed by synthetic biology, adverse effects on the environment, and other such harm;

3. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Science and ICT, such as guidelines for specific technical fields to ensure the safety of synthetic biology research and development.

Article 18 (Establishment and Operation of a Safety Management System, Etc.)

(1) The Minister of Science and ICT shall perform the following duties to establish and operate the safety management system under Article 26 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "safety management system"):

1. Formulating the basic direction of the safety management system;

2. Formulating research and development guidelines and inspecting and managing compliance with them;

3. Formulating safety management measures for each research field in synthetic biology;

4. Preparing detailed safety management measures for each area of responsibility of the relevant central administrative agencies;

5. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Science and ICT for the establishment and operation of the safety management system.

(2) When implementing national research and development projects related to synthetic biology, the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies shall include matters concerning research and development guidelines in agreements for research and development tasks under Article 11 of the National Research and Development Innovation Act. In such cases, where research and development guidelines are violated or hazards arise in the course of implementing a national research and development project, the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies may change the research and development objectives, principal investigator, or other such matters of a research and development task, or discontinue the relevant research and development task, in conjunction with a special evaluation under Article 15 (1) of the National Research and Development Innovation Act.

(3) Matters necessary for the establishment and operation of the safety management system, etc., other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

Article 19 (Methods and Procedures for Safety Management Monitoring, Etc.)

(1) The heads of the relevant central administrative agencies shall regularly conduct safety management monitoring under Article 26 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "safety management monitoring" in this Article) of research and development institutions carrying out research and development tasks related to synthetic biology (meaning research and development institutions selected under Article 10 of the National Research and Development Innovation Act; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article), at intervals prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT. In such cases, the heads of the relevant research and development institutions shall be informed in advance of the safety management monitoring plan and procedures.

(2) Where hazards are discovered or the fulfillment of safety management obligations is deemed inadequate as a result of safety management monitoring, the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies shall request the heads of the relevant research and development institutions to take necessary measures, such as correction or improvement, and may conduct additional inspections to verify whether those measures have been implemented.

(3) The heads of research and development institutions shall conduct safety management inspections from time to time and regularly report the results to the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies. However, where an urgent hazard, such as a safety accident, arises, they shall immediately report it to the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies.

(4) The heads of the relevant central administrative agencies shall notify the Minister of Science and ICT of the results of measures under paragraph (2) and the contents of reports under paragraph (3), and the Minister of Science and ICT shall compile this information and reflect it in the establishment and operation of the safety management system.

(5) Matters necessary for measures based on the results of safety management monitoring and for procedures and responses in emergencies, etc., other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT.

CHAPTER 6 SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 20 (Entrustment of Duties)

(1) Pursuant to Article 29 (2) of the Act, the following duties of the Minister of Science and ICT shall be entrusted to the specialized institution for synthetic biology policy under Article 13 (1) of the Act:

1. Surveys of current conditions and outlooks and forecasts for the direction of development under Article 9 (1) of the Act;

2. Compilation of statistics under Article 10 (1) of the Act;

3. Technology impact assessments under Article 11 (1) of the Act and technology level assessments under paragraph (2) of that Article.

(2) Pursuant to Article 29 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Science and ICT may entrust the following duties to institutions or organizations publicly notified by the Minister of Science and ICT, taking into account the organizational structure, personnel, expertise, and other factors necessary for the performance of those duties:

1. Receipt and review of applications for designation as hub institutions for synthetic biology research and development under Article 15 (1) of the Act;

2. Receipt and review of applications for designation as operating institutions under Article 19 (2) of the Act;

3. Support for the formulation of synthetic biology research and development guidelines under Article 25 (1) of the Act;

4. Support for the establishment and operation of the safety management system under Article 26 (1) of the Act.

(3) Where the Minister of Science and ICT entrusts duties pursuant to paragraph (2), the Minister shall publicly notify the entrusted institution and the details of the entrusted duties.

ADDENDA <No. 36265, 2026.4.21>

This Decree shall enter into force on April 23, 2026.
